# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 3 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r2 has "¬◇infinite"; aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.2 has "□(ultimate → intermediate) ∧ □(intermediate → first)"; they differ when only "necessarily, (if there is an ultimate effect (ultimate cause), then there is an intermediate efficient cause)" is true, which the first allows; aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.3 has nothing from it
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S4 is read aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.3 with no modality; aquinas-second-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.2 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "necessarily, (if there is an ultimate effect (ultimate cause), then there is an intermediate efficient cause)" is true and "necessarily, (if there is an intermediate efficient cause, then there is a first efficient cause)" and "possibly, in efficient causes one goes on to infinity" are false. Is S4, "Now in efficient causes it is not possible to go on to infinity, because in all efficient causes following in order, the first is the cause of the intermediate cause, and the intermediate is the cause of the ultimate cause, whether the intermediate cause be several, or only one.", true then?
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r2 has "(¬first → ¬intermediate) ∧ (¬intermediate → ¬ultimate)"; aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.2 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.3 have "□(¬first → ¬intermediate) ∧ □(¬intermediate → ¬ultimate)"; they differ when only "there is a first efficient cause", "there is an intermediate efficient cause", "there is an ultimate effect (ultimate cause)" and "necessarily, (if it is not so that there is an intermediate efficient cause, then it is not so that there is an ultimate effect (ultimate cause))" are true, which the first allows
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S5 is read aquinas-second-way.logic.r2 with no modality; aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.2 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.3 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.2 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "there is a first efficient cause", "there is an intermediate efficient cause", "there is an ultimate effect (ultimate cause)", "necessarily, (if it is not so that there is an intermediate efficient cause, then it is not so that there is an ultimate effect (ultimate cause))" and "necessarily, (if it is not so that there is an intermediate efficient cause, then it is not so that there is an ultimate effect (ultimate cause))" are true and "necessarily, (if it is not so that there is a first efficient cause, then it is not so that there is an intermediate efficient cause)" and "necessarily, (if it is not so that there is a first efficient cause, then it is not so that there is an intermediate efficient cause)" are false. Is S5, "Now to take away the cause is to take away the effect.", true then?
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/readings: S7 is read 2 ways: aquinas-second-way.logic.r2 has "infinite → ¬first ∧ ¬ultimate ∧ ¬intermediate" and "ultimate ∧ intermediate"; aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.2 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.3 have "□(infinite → ¬first)" and "ultimate ∧ intermediate"; they differ when only "there is a first efficient cause", "there is an intermediate efficient cause" and "there is an ultimate effect (ultimate cause)" are true, which the first allows
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S7 is read aquinas-second-way.logic.r2 with no modality; aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.2 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.3 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means
aquinas-second-way.md:1: argue/answered: S7: answered no, which keeps aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.2 and aquinas-second-way.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "there is a first efficient cause", "there is an intermediate efficient cause" and "there is an ultimate effect (ultimate cause)" are true and "in efficient causes one goes on to infinity", "necessarily, (if in efficient causes one goes on to infinity, then it is not so that there is a first efficient cause)" and "necessarily, (if in efficient causes one goes on to infinity, then it is not so that there is a first efficient cause)" are false. Is S7, "But if in efficient causes it is possible to go on to infinity, there will be no first efficient cause, neither will there be an ultimate effect, nor any intermediate efficient causes; all of which is plainly false.", true then?
